<Fragment slot="head">
<script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={`{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://www.physical-model.com/faq#webpage","url":"https://www.physical-model.com/faq","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://www.physical-model.com/#website"},"about":{"@id":"https://www.physical-model.com/#organization"},"mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of models do you make?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We create architectural and real estate models, product prototypes and mockups, film and commercial miniatures, and art maquettes and prototypes. This includes massing models, masterplans, planning models, sales-gallery models, topographic and site models, appearance prototypes, functional mockups, miniature sets, sculpture studies, and presentation models. Every project is developed around its intended audience, use, required level of detail, physical constraints, schedule, and delivery requirements."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Who do you work with?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We work with architects, developers, planners, general contractors, design consultants, product teams, film and commercial production teams, agencies, artists, and cultural institutions across the United States and Canada. Our models support design evaluation, planning and stakeholder approvals, presentations, testing, filming, exhibitions, and other situations where an idea needs to be clearly understood in physical form."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Why use a physical model instead of renderings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Renderings present selected views of a design. A physical model allows people to examine scale, massing, proportion, circulation, context, and spatial relationships together from multiple viewpoints. It can reduce ambiguity across technical and nontechnical audiences and reveal questions that may be less apparent on a screen."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is an architectural scale model?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An architectural scale model is a physical representation of a building, interior, site, or larger development at a reduced scale. It can communicate massing, spatial relationships, circulation, context, façades, materials, landscaping, lighting, and other aspects of a design."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What files do you need to start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We can begin a review with CAD, BIM, Revit, Rhino, SketchUp, STL or OBJ files, PDFs, dimensioned drawings, sketches, photographs, renderings, reference images, or an existing physical object. Complete production-ready geometry is not required for the initial review."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you work from an AI-generated image or rendering?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. An AI-generated image, rendering, photograph, or sketch can provide a useful starting point, but it may not contain enough dimensional or construction information for physical fabrication. We review the available material and determine whether separately scoped Digital Model Development is needed."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What if I do not have a 3D model?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A 3D model is not required for the initial review. We can begin with drawings, sketches, photographs, renderings, reference images, dimensions, or an existing object."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Digital Model Development a separate service?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Preparing usable client-provided geometry for fabrication is part of the model-making process. Creating new geometry from sketches, photographs, renderings, or reference images requires additional design interpretation and is therefore scoped separately."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get an estimate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Send us your project details, intended use, desired dimensions or scale, schedule, delivery location, and any available files or reference material. We will review the information and provide a preliminary estimate or recommend the next step required to define the scope."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does an architectural model cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Carve Model projects start at US$4,000. Final pricing depends on the model's dimensions and scale, level of detail, source information, materials, finishes, lighting, display requirements, schedule, packaging, transportation, and installation."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What scale should my model be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The appropriate scale depends on the actual size of the subject, what the audience needs to understand, the required level of detail, viewing distance, available display space, transportation constraints, and final use."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What level of detail can you achieve?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We produce everything from simplified massing models to highly detailed presentation models with façade treatments, glazing, landscaping, interiors, custom finishes, and integrated lighting."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a model take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Many projects can be completed in approximately two to four weeks after the scope and design information are confirmed. Larger, highly detailed, illuminated, interactive, or technically complex models may require additional time."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if the design changes during production?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We assess how the proposed change affects approved geometry, materials, completed work, schedule, and cost. Minor changes may be incorporated within the existing workflow; substantial changes may require a revised scope, price, or delivery date."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are Carve models produced?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Carve operates as one coordinated model-making team across Los Angeles and China. Our Los Angeles team manages client communication, project planning, approvals, and delivery, while our established fabrication team carries out production. Every stage — from design review and fabrication to quality control, packaging, and shipping — is managed through a single Carve process."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What materials and production methods do you use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Materials are selected according to the model's purpose, scale, appearance, structural requirements, handling, and transportation needs, and may include acrylic, ABS, resin, wood, metal, foam, board, printed components, landscaping materials, lighting, and custom finishes."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Does every project require physical samples or mockups?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Samples and mockups are used when physical confirmation would materially reduce uncertainty. Simpler projects may proceed through digital and documented approvals without a separate physical sample."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you ensure quality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Quality control begins before fabrication with an approved scope, scale, materials, finishes, and design information. Key production stages are reviewed for accuracy, appearance, assembly, lighting, function, and stability."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I track my project's progress?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Every Carve project includes access to the Carve Studio Portal, where you can review milestones, exchange files and messages, provide approvals, and see the current status of your project in one organized workspace."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"How are project files and confidential information handled?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Project files, approvals, and communications are organized within the Carve Studio Portal and made available to the people involved in delivering the project. NDA or restricted access requirements can be established before confidential material is sent."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you ship throughout the United States and Canada?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Carve coordinates delivery throughout the United States and Canada. Once a model has passed final quality control, it typically ships directly from our production facility to the client's delivery location. Direct shipment reduces unnecessary handling, shortens transit time, and lowers the risk of damage. Packaging is tailored to the model's size, fragility, and handling requirements, with on-site assembly and installation available when needed."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if my model is damaged during shipping?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Carve coordinates the shipping process and remains your primary point of contact if an issue occurs during delivery. If a model arrives damaged, notify us promptly and provide photographs of the packaging and affected areas. We will assess the damage and coordinate an appropriate resolution, which may include repair, replacement, or another solution based on the project and delivery terms."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can large models be divided for transportation or installation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. When necessary, the model and its base can be designed in sections to accommodate shipping limits, doorways, elevators, building access, handling, and final assembly."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you offer installation and setup?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Depending on the project, a model can be delivered ready for client setup or Carve can coordinate on-site assembly, installation, lighting connections, testing, and final placement."}}
]}`} />
<style>
.page-hero{padding-top:clamp(3rem,6vw,5rem);padding-bottom:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem);border-bottom:1px solid var(--cm-line-faint);}
.page-hero h1{margin-top:1.4rem;}.page-hero p{margin-top:1.4rem;}
.faq-wrap{display:grid;grid-template-columns:0.5fr 1fr;gap:clamp(2rem,5vw,4rem);}
.faq-cat{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:0.72rem;letter-spacing:0.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--cm-silver);margin:2.5rem 0 0.5rem;}
.faq-cat:first-child{margin-top:0;}
.faq-list{border-top:1px solid var(--cm-line-faint);}
.faq-item{border-bottom:1px solid var(--cm-line-faint);}
.faq-item summary{list-style:none;cursor:pointer;padding:1.4rem 0;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:2rem;align-items:center;font-family:var(--font-display);font-weight:var(--w-semibold);font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--cm-text-strong);}
.faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none;}
.faq-item .pm{position:relative;width:16px;height:16px;flex:none;}
.faq-item .pm::before,.faq-item .pm::after{content:"";position:absolute;background:var(--cm-silver);transition:transform var(--dur-3);}
.faq-item .pm::before{top:7.5px;left:0;width:16px;height:1.5px;}
.faq-item .pm::after{top:0;left:7.5px;width:1.5px;height:16px;}
.faq-item[open] .pm::after{transform:scaleY(0);}
.faq-item p{padding:0 0 1rem;color:var(--cm-text-muted);max-width:70ch;}
.faq-item p:last-of-type{padding-bottom:1.5rem;}
.faq-aside{position:sticky;top:100px;align-self:start;background:var(--cm-ink-900);border:1px solid var(--cm-border);border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:1.8rem;}
.faq-aside h3{font-size:1.2rem;}.faq-aside p{margin-top:0.7rem;color:var(--cm-text-muted);font-size:0.92rem;}
.faq-aside .btn--primary,.faq-aside .btn--ghost{background:transparent;color:var(--cm-text-strong);border:1px solid var(--cm-border-strong);}
.faq-aside .btn--primary:hover,.faq-aside .btn--ghost:hover{background:var(--cm-accent);color:var(--cm-accent-contrast);border-color:var(--cm-accent);}
@media(max-width:820px){.faq-wrap{grid-template-columns:1fr;}.faq-aside{position:static;}}
</style>
</Fragment>

<main>
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container">
  <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
  <h1 class="display-lg measure">Questions, answered.</h1>
  <p class="lead measure">Practical information about starting, defining, producing, and delivering a physical model or prototype. If you are unsure what your project requires, send us the information you have and we will help define the appropriate next step.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="container faq-wrap">
  <div>
    <div class="faq-cat">Models and applications</div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What types of models do you make? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>We create architectural and real estate models, product prototypes and mockups, film and commercial miniatures, and art maquettes and prototypes. This includes massing models, masterplans, planning models, sales-gallery models, topographic and site models, appearance prototypes, functional mockups, miniature sets, sculpture studies, and presentation models.</p><p>Every project is developed around its intended audience, use, required level of detail, physical constraints, schedule, and delivery requirements.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Who do you work with? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>We work with architects, developers, planners, general contractors, design consultants, product teams, film and commercial production teams, agencies, artists, and cultural institutions across the United States and Canada.</p><p>Our models support design evaluation, planning and stakeholder approvals, presentations, testing, filming, exhibitions, and other situations where an idea needs to be clearly understood in physical form.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Why use a physical model instead of renderings? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Renderings present selected views of a design. A physical model allows people to examine scale, massing, proportion, circulation, context, and spatial relationships together from multiple viewpoints.</p><p>It can reduce ambiguity across technical and nontechnical audiences and reveal questions that may be less apparent on a screen. Physical models and renderings can complement each other; the right format depends on what the audience needs to understand, evaluate, or decide.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What is an architectural scale model? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>An architectural scale model is a physical representation of a building, interior, site, or larger development at a reduced scale. It can communicate massing, spatial relationships, circulation, context, façades, materials, landscaping, lighting, and other aspects of a design.</p><p>Architectural models are commonly used for design reviews, planning approvals, stakeholder meetings, investor presentations, sales galleries, exhibitions, and as-built documentation.</p></details>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-cat">Files and design readiness</div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What files do you need to start? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>We can begin a review with CAD, BIM, Revit, Rhino, SketchUp, STL or OBJ files, PDFs, dimensioned drawings, sketches, photographs, renderings, reference images, or an existing physical object.</p><p>Complete production-ready geometry is not required for the initial review. If usable geometry is unavailable, we will identify the additional information and separately scoped Digital Model Development required before fabrication.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Can you work from an AI-generated image or rendering? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. An AI-generated image, rendering, photograph, or sketch can provide a useful starting point, but it may not contain enough dimensional or construction information for physical fabrication.</p><p>We review the available material, identify what must be clarified, and determine whether separately scoped Digital Model Development is needed to create suitable geometry.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What if I do not have a 3D model? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>A 3D model is not required for the initial review. We can begin with drawings, sketches, photographs, renderings, reference images, dimensions, or an existing object.</p><p>If fabrication-ready geometry does not exist, we will explain what additional information or Digital Model Development is required before the physical model can be produced.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Is Digital Model Development a separate service? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Preparing usable client-provided geometry for fabrication is part of the model-making process. Creating new geometry from sketches, photographs, renderings, or reference images requires additional design interpretation and is therefore scoped separately.</p><p>The developed geometry is reviewed and approved before physical fabrication proceeds. Digital Model Development is intended to create the coordinated information required for the physical deliverable.</p></details>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-cat">Scope, pricing and schedule</div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How do I get an estimate? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Send us your project details, intended use, desired dimensions or scale, schedule, delivery location, and any available files or reference material.</p><p>We will review the information, identify any questions, and provide a preliminary estimate or recommend the next step required to define the scope. Once the requirements are confirmed, we provide a formal quotation and proposed timeline before fabrication begins.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How much does an architectural model cost? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Carve Model projects start at US$4,000. Final pricing depends on the model's dimensions and scale, level of detail, source information, materials, finishes, lighting, display requirements, schedule, packaging, transportation, and installation.</p><p>Because every model is custom-built, a project-specific price can only be confirmed after we review the intended use and scope.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What scale should my model be? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>The appropriate scale depends on the actual size of the subject, what the audience needs to understand, the required level of detail, viewing distance, available display space, transportation constraints, and final use.</p><p>Small scales are generally appropriate for masterplans, context, and overall massing. Larger scales allow more architectural, interior, material, and functional detail. We calculate the resulting model dimensions and recommend an appropriate scale during project review.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What level of detail can you achieve? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>We produce everything from simplified massing models to highly detailed presentation models with façade treatments, glazing, landscaping, interiors, custom finishes, and integrated lighting.</p><p>The appropriate level of detail is determined by what the model needs to communicate. We define what should be represented accurately, what should be emphasized, and what can be simplified to make the model clearer and more effective.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How long does a model take? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Many projects can be completed in approximately two to four weeks after the scope and design information are confirmed. Larger, highly detailed, illuminated, interactive, or technically complex models may require additional time.</p><p>The schedule also depends on client review, revisions, material approvals, shipping, and installation requirements. A project-specific timeline is provided before fabrication begins.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What happens if the design changes during production? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>We assess how the proposed change affects approved geometry, materials, completed work, schedule, and cost.</p><p>Minor changes may be incorporated within the existing workflow. Substantial changes may require a revised scope, price, or delivery date. We document the change and its effects for client approval before proceeding.</p></details>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-cat">Production, review and quality</div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Where are Carve models produced? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Carve operates as one coordinated model-making team across Los Angeles and China. Our Los Angeles team manages client communication, project planning, approvals, and delivery, while our established fabrication team carries out production.</p><p>Every stage — from design review and fabrication to quality control, packaging, and shipping — is managed through a single Carve process.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What materials and production methods do you use? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Materials are selected according to the model's purpose, scale, appearance, structural requirements, handling, and transportation needs.</p><p>Depending on the project, we may use acrylic, ABS, resin, wood, metal, foam, board, printed components, landscaping materials, lighting, and custom finishes. Fabrication can combine laser cutting, CNC machining, 3D printing, painting, assembly, and skilled handcraft.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Does every project require physical samples or mockups? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>No. Samples and mockups are used when physical confirmation would materially reduce uncertainty — for example, when approving colors, materials, finishes, lighting, connections, moving components, or important details.</p><p>Simpler projects may proceed through digital and documented approvals without a separate physical sample. We recommend the appropriate approval method based on the project's complexity and risk.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How do you ensure quality? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Quality control begins before fabrication with an approved scope, scale, materials, finishes, and design information.</p><p>Key production stages are reviewed for accuracy, appearance, assembly, lighting, function, and stability. When required, samples or mockups are approved before full fabrication. Progress and quality-control information are shared through the Carve Studio Portal, and the completed model is inspected before packaging and delivery.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I track my project's progress? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. Every Carve project includes access to the Carve Studio Portal, where you can review milestones, exchange files and messages, provide approvals, and see the current status of your project in one organized workspace.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>How are project files and confidential information handled? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Project files, approvals, and communications are organized within the Carve Studio Portal and made available to the people involved in delivering the project.</p><p>If your project requires an NDA, restricted file access, or specific information-handling procedures, let us know before sending confidential material so the appropriate requirements can be established.</p></details>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-cat">Delivery and installation</div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Do you ship throughout the United States and Canada? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. Carve coordinates delivery throughout the United States and Canada. Once a model has passed final quality control, it typically ships directly from our production facility to the client's delivery location.</p><p>Direct shipment reduces unnecessary handling, shortens transit time, and lowers the risk of damage. Packaging is tailored to the model's size, fragility, and handling requirements, with on-site assembly and installation available when needed.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What happens if my model is damaged during shipping? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Carve coordinates the shipping process and remains your primary point of contact if an issue occurs during delivery.</p><p>If a model arrives damaged, notify us promptly and provide photographs of the packaging and affected areas. We will assess the damage and coordinate an appropriate resolution, which may include repair, replacement, or another solution based on the project and delivery terms.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Can large models be divided for transportation or installation? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. When necessary, the model and its base can be designed in sections to accommodate shipping limits, doorways, elevators, building access, handling, and final assembly.</p><p>These constraints should be identified during project scoping so they can be incorporated before the model is designed for production.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Do you offer installation and setup? <span class="pm"></span></summary><p>Yes. Depending on the project, a model can be delivered ready for client setup or Carve can coordinate on-site assembly, installation, lighting connections, testing, and final placement.</p><p>Installation requirements, site access, available power, scheduling, and responsibilities are defined during project scoping.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
  <aside class="faq-aside">
    <h3>Still have a question?</h3>
    <p>Tell us what you are creating, who needs to understand it, and what the model needs to help them evaluate, communicate, test, or approve. We will review the information and recommend an appropriate next step.</p>
    <a class="btn btn--primary full mt-3" href="/contact" style="margin-top:1.3rem;">Get a Preliminary Estimate →</a>
    <a class="btn btn--ghost full mt-2" href="mailto:support@physical-model.com" style="margin-top:0.7rem;">Email the Studio →</a>
  </aside>
</div></section>
</main>
</BaseLayout>
